³1ip³2217.247.142.155³2ts³21095193048³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3small addition³3text³3= Utilitarianism and <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> =

&nbsp;


Utilitarianism. Two types-- General Utilitarianism & Rule Utilitarianism. See Hazlitt's online book.

Generally, if one can predict a net gain in utility (to self or to the human race), then the action is to be taken...regardless of any moral stance. Or rulewise, if one can devise a rule that if everyone abided by that rule, one can predict results of a net gain in utility (to the rule abiders or the human race), then the action is to be taken...regardless of any moral stance.

The trick of predicting or defining 'utility' is said to be the weakness here. But, remember that day-to-day, humans do that anyway as best they can. One cannot predict to the end of time. In any case, read Hazlitt as he might make more sense of it.

